# The Progeny Bedchamber!
Tamil title: சந்தான சப்ரமஞ்சம்!

“Your Majesty! If you and your lawful wife eat this mango on the night when the full moon shines, you will certainly be blessed with a son.”

“Great sage! I am blessed! A thousand crore salutations for your boon.”

This is news from the age of the Puranas.

“Go to the temple at Virinjipuram, bathe in the sacred tank, and lie down that night in the temple hall in your wet clothes. Even a barren woman will receive the boon of a son.”

This is news from here, even today.

“Pay a fee of fifty sovereigns and spend one night in the progeny-bedchamber hall. Even couples who have long remained childless will have a baby.”

This is news from London in 1779.

The first two statements are familiar and readily understood. Readers will eagerly ask us to explain the third: what was this progeny-bedchamber hall? Dear friends, we cannot rush straight to that stage. Before reaching it, we must learn how humankind passed through the stages of magic, incantation, and medicine, and what experiences it encountered.

The man who gave the mango was a great sage. The man who built the progeny bed was not a sage but a white man.

Though they were different men, their purpose was the same.

The king believed that the sage's mango possessed greatness. The common man believed that the mattress held a power capable of granting children.

Both beliefs arose because knowledge had not developed. This article may be taken as a commentary upon that history.

“We tried every treatment and saw no result. The child's life was hanging by a thread. The doctors said there was no hope and that he would not survive the next day. What could I do? Nothing came to mind. At once I prayed, ‘Lord of the Seven Hills! You alone are my refuge. Every human being has abandoned us; now you alone must grant this life as alms. When the child survives, I shall bring him to your shrine and offer a silver leg.’ I tied a quarter-anna in a piece of turmeric cloth, fastened a sacred thread around his wrist, placed the burden upon the Lord of the Seven Hills, and lay beside the child.

“It must have been midnight. My eyes closed and I slept for a little while. In a dream an old man with a large Vaishnavite mark upon his forehead seemed to tap me awake. ‘What is this, woman? You summon me and then lie snoring! Get up and give the child milk. I have saved him,’ he said. I awoke suddenly and, just look at the miracle, the child asked, ‘Mother, give me milk or coffee; I am terribly hungry!’ Crying, ‘Lord of the Seven Hills, such is your glory!’ I ran, made coffee, and brought it to him. He swallowed four mouthfuls. The fever fell at once, and by cockcrow his body was no longer burning. Within four days he sat up. What do you say to that? Forty days of fever, four doctors, and every medicine one could give: that poisonous fever yielded to none of them. The moment we uttered the Lord's name and tied the sacred thread, it fled at once!”

“That is no great miracle, woman! Was not our Ekambaram's wife wasting away to a skeleton? She had climbed up and down the steps of every hospital. Ekambaram brought all kinds of medicines from the city without caring about money. Her intestines had been ruined by taking medicine upon medicine. Everyone said she could never survive and that this was all the good fortune he had been allotted.

“Thoppai, who worked in their garden, came like a god and said, ‘Do not dismiss my suggestion because I am poor. Go to the mosque on our Big Street for fifteen days at the time worship ends. Those coming out after prayer will blow “oos, oos” upon her face. Whatever the disease, it will fly away like scattered cotton. Try it.’ Would a poor man's words ever reach the public platform? They mocked him: ‘Fool! Can breath from the mouths of those leaving a mosque accomplish what such mighty doctors could not?’ Ekambaram nevertheless decided they might as well try it and took her there. On precisely the tenth day her body acquired a fresh glow. I have just seen her. There is no trace of illness; she looks like a pineapple! Is that not a miracle? Six hundred rupees had already been spent on medicine. The mosque cost not one quarter-anna, yet the illness vanished. What do you say to that?”

“Yes, indeed! Do such miracles not occur? Our Veerasami decided he must take a second wife because eight years of marriage had produced no child. He bought tonic after tonic for Damayanti. She grew as strong as Sandow, but there was no baby. ‘It is our fate; what can we do?’ she lamented. Then Thoppai, the gardener, arrived like a god. ‘Why worry, mother? Fulfil a vow at the Virinjipuram temple and the child you desire will be born,’ he said.

“She told her husband, ‘Let us try this one method too. If it brings no result, I swear I shall myself give you my younger sister as a second wife.’ They went to Virinjipuram. One must bathe there, lie all night in the temple hall in a wet sari, and the deity will appear in a dream. ‘Lord, we asked that a flower should bloom,’ one says, and the deity grants the boon, saying, ‘The flower shall bloom.’ Some receive it on the first day; others must return for months. Each gets only as much as her accumulated merit allows. Her fortune brought the boon on the seventh day at Virinjipuram. The baby is now six months old and beautifully plump; they have named him Virinjiyappan. What do you say? No medicine, injection, diet, or oil. Lie in wet clothes and pray with reverence: that is enough! Is it not a miracle?”

Sacred wrist threads, knotted cords, ritual immersions, and amulets: not merely old women but even many educated people who enjoy the facilities and opportunities created by innovation in other fields say and believe that these things cure incurable diseases. They even offer “arguments” to unbelievers.

When old women say any disease can be cured through magic, incantation, and divine favour, we might ask: “Grandmother, if that is true and disease can be cured so easily, why spend crores building new hospitals and lakhs buying medicines? Why employ thousands of highly paid doctors? If childbirth requires Virinjipuram, demonic possession Samayapuram, stomach ache Vallipuram, fever Kasipuram, and cough Dharapuram, why not send each patient wherever the appropriate miracle resides and cure everyone in an instant? Why should medicine require education and a degree? Why should hospitals contain so many doctors?”

They do not come forward to debate heroically. They look at us affectionately and say, “Son, I cannot explain how any of that works. I do not understand its meaning. All I know is that those miracles occur. Have I spent thousands upon thousands and studied as you have, so that I might understand the rest?” We may even respect those old women for their innocent hearts and honest faith.

But others scatter terms such as operation, inoculation, injection, radium treatment, X-ray, and ultraviolet ray whenever medicine is discussed. They proudly describe the skill of Dr Rangachari or the great operation performed by Dr Rajan. Like junior doctors, they list the medicines for each disease and the vitamins contained in each substance. After displaying all this cleverness, in the same breath they become impassioned advocates for grandmother's methods: sacred threads and cords, ritual baths, and the driving out of spirits. They even deliver a verdict in their favour.

The position has become one of magic, incantation, and medicine, *mani-mantra-aushadha*. Before medicine could develop, however, new knowledge had to struggle greatly under the assault of stupidity.

Circling temples or trees for a child, immersing oneself in a tank, and tying on a talisman existed not only here but in many countries before the rays of knowledge arose.

The new understanding that disease is the effect of germs entering the body has emerged only recently. For long ages, people here and elsewhere believed illness came from a god's anger or an evil spirit's mischief. They therefore believed disease could be removed by driving away the spirit or pleasing the god. In those days the priest was the doctor and the temple the hospital. Today there is an effort to turn temples into hospitals, and even an effort to build small temples in corners of hospitals.

The grip upon those who board trains for pilgrimage to Rameswaram remains strong. There are people who fly in, witness a local float festival, board another aircraft, and offer counsel at the United Nations! When we speak of magic, incantation, and medicine, in other countries the words denote three stages of development. Here they are mixed and administered together.

Those who want clarity must read the history of how intellectuals abroad struggled to reach the conclusion that medicine, not magic or incantation, cures disease; how much research they conducted, how many scientific truths were discovered, and how greatly those discoveries reduced human suffering and brought comfort and happiness. An article can only awaken an appetite for that history; it cannot provide the entire history.

There was a time when skulls, animal teeth and claws, and various rings were instruments for “curing disease.” Today the method is to protect the body and destroy the germs that consume it.

People who roamed forests, lived in caves, and ate raw flesh believed that wearing a garland of skulls brought the grace of the god of death; no illness would approach one enjoying that grace because no evil spirit would dare possess him. Forest became country, cave became palace, and clothing, food, and systems of government all changed. Yet the ancient belief that a skull garland is a great medicine refuses to disappear completely.

Its remnants survive when wealthy people say a necklace of nine gems wards off particular diseases; Saivites say no illness will approach one who wears sacred ash and rudraksha beads; Vaishnavites say evil and disease will not touch one who wears a sacred tulasi garland; and the next group says a black cord with 108 knots keeps away 1,008 ailments. Puranic preachers ask rationalists to accept such deception.

Like the Puranic storytellers of this country, those of Greece and Rome were highly skilled at turning cosmic falsehoods into delightful tales, making those tales the will of gods, and confusing people's minds. So much mythology writhed there that Socrates, worthy of being called the first rationalist, had to arise in Greece.

The mythmakers of ancient Greece and Rome were not content merely to say that gods or demons caused disease and that worship, fasting, and vows would cure it. They assigned a separate deity to every illness. You might appeal all day to a god who could cure pain in the eye, but he would not cure pain in the leg: that department had been assigned to another god! By allocating one god to each disease, a priestly crowd and army of frauds squeezed devotees dry and grew fat.

Greece first tried to drive away the darkness covering people's minds. Rome, meanwhile, cut down and heaped up the heads of other peoples while devising plans to rule the world under a single umbrella. Even after the light of rational thought spread through Greece, Rome lay at the feet of intellectual blindness.

Just as the treasures of thought Greece gave the world, Socrates, Plato, and Aristotle, are still regarded and celebrated as teachers and guides in their fields, the man still honoured as the foremost figure in medicine was also Greek: Hippocrates.

Hippocrates shone as an emperor of medicine. The discoveries he made and the standards he upheld remain ideals for the medical profession.

Many trained under him and practised medicine skilfully. Especially commendable were the procedures he devised for making childbirth safe. He trained midwives in methods of safe delivery.

Those ignorant of medicine used terrifying and cruel methods in childbirth.

They imagined that the child in the womb struggled to emerge because it was extremely hungry. They therefore piled many foods and sweets before the pregnant woman, believing the fragrance would draw the child out. That at least might be considered amusing. Many other methods were cruel.

Thinking the child in the womb was playing a trick by refusing to come out, they reportedly lifted the pregnant woman, turned her upside down, and shook her, as we might shake a bottle to dislodge a cork trapped inside. Some believed tying her to a tree and hanging her produced results. Others climbed upon her abdomen and stamped upon it to bring about delivery. Mountain people possessed a still more terrible method surpassing all these. They tied the pregnant woman to a post in a field while a horseman charged from the opposite side as though he would trample her. Fright was supposed to induce labour.

Hippocrates changed such conditions and introduced medical methods, not after enlightenment had spread widely, but when blind beliefs enveloped society, around 450 BCE.

The myth about the god of medicine itself shows the superstitions current in Hippocrates' time.

Pluto, god of death, knelt before the great god Zeus and complained, “Great Lord! Not many people come to my realm now. You must provide a remedy.”

The great god considered the cause and understood it.

Asclepius, son of the god Apollo, knew medical methods and was removing people's fear of death. With his help they escaped dangerous diseases, and the death toll had fallen greatly.

Is that not good? One might say Asclepius, who prevented death, ought to be worshipped as a god. The Greeks did so. They built a temple for the god of medicine and conducted splendid festivals.

The Greeks worshipped him, but the Greek gods became furious with him. Would the other gods enjoy seeing the god of medicine receive more worship than all of them and wield enormous influence? There was jealousy and anger, especially from the god of death, because fewer and fewer people entered his realm.

After hearing Pluto's complaint, Zeus hurled his thunderbolt and killed the god of medicine, hoping the dominion of death would again prevail on earth.

The priestly teaching was that people who lost their fear of death would forget the gods. Thus they invented this “holy” tale to say that even if one god tried to abolish death, the supreme god would not leave him alone.

The sick came to the temple where the god of medicine sat enthroned. Priests prescribed various rituals and received offerings. If the illness did not end, at least the patient would die, and they would rest content saying it was the will of god.

Greek thinkers later converted just such temples into medical institutions.

Greece advanced in rational thought. Rome gloried in victories on the battlefield and used the wealth obtained there to conduct magnificent festivals. Whether a flower garden lacked new blossoms or women failed to bear children, whatever the deficiency, people worshipped some god and asked for a boon.

Ignoring such condemnation, Greek physicians continued their work and succeeded. Medical practice became established in Rome too. Then another danger arose.

Christianity gained boundless influence in Europe, and a sickness prevailed in which the word of guardians of the faith, especially monasteries, was itself scripture. Whenever a researcher offered a new idea in any field, the monks knitted their brows and said: “Ignorant men! The Lord who rules the world has already revealed everything and given all that must be given. Instead of remaining satisfied, you speak of research, babble about new ideas, and wander in search of new things. This is a great error and mortal sin.”

Researchers had to live in fear. The Pope, reigning as head of religion, possessed authority to curse them as great sinners, and many realms bowed to that authority.

In the early days, when Greece spread the rays of rational thought, Rome had mocked and opposed it and remained a camp of orthodoxy. That same city of Rome became the temple of a Pope determined that new knowledge must be crushed to preserve holiness.

Childbirth was dangerous enough to take women's lives, and the pain mothers endured was beyond description. Medical researchers therefore sought a way to reduce it. They conducted many investigations, particularly to ease childbirth and generally to prevent extreme pain during surgery. Medical experts proposed giving patients an anaesthetic and operating while they were unconscious, and suitable drugs were prepared.

Religious advocates refused to accept this. “It is the Lord's will that she experience pain. Who are you, fool, to alter it?” they roared.

They cited scripture as evidence.

The Bible contains a reference to giving birth in pain. When the Lord had spoken thus, was it not sinful to prevent the pregnant woman from feeling pain by administering an anaesthetic? Would God permit it? Those whom medical education had turned into great sinners demanded painless childbirth, while scripture said pain must be experienced. Had these scoundrels more compassion for humanity than the Lord? Could researchers know something God did not? How arrogant must they be to alter what scripture said? The guardians of religion fiercely opposed relief from childbirth pain as naked atheism.

Reading of such opposition today, we may laugh and ask whether this could truly have caused a dispute. But the question of whether childbirth ought to be painful or pain ought to be removed sent sparks through universities and assemblies of priests and ignited controversies, debates, and hostilities throughout the land.

A priest might explain it this way:

“Devotees! Devotees unwilling to lose God's grace, listen! Scripture says childbirth must be painful; those who recite it require no evidence. Yet these researchers strive to wipe away that pain. In the name of the Lord and our holy faith, I ask them: why does the Lord say mothers must suffer in childbirth? Would God give humanity something it did not need?

“Have these researchers examined the reason? They peer through magnifying glasses at germs! Their other researches need only a dead frog, rotten fruit, or decaying body. The investigation I demand requires a heart overflowing with devotion and faith in the Lord. These great sinners possess neither; therefore they cannot understand his word.

“The Lord declares categorically that labour pains must exist. Why? Because pain spreads devotion. As the woman writhes in agony, how many thousands of times does she pray in the Lord's name and invoke Mother Mary?

“‘Lord, you are my refuge! Lord, save me! Jesus, come and end my pain. Precious Mother Mary, show mercy to this sinner.’

“Devotion rises from her heart. Not only the suffering woman but her husband and parents suffer at seeing her. They too pray. Everyone invokes the Lord's name. The delivery room becomes a hall of worship. All attention turns to God, and the understanding arises that he has authority over everything. The creator shows that he can make her writhe, and faith arises that if his name is repeated, he can also save her.

“In general, labour pain helps everyone acquire renewed faith in scripture. It is holy work that spreads the Lord's fame and devotion. In church we receive devotion through music; in maternity hospitals, cries of pain further kindle it. Devotion blazes forth!

“That is why the Lord said childbirth must be painful. What do you now say of these contentious men?”

The priest would ask this after his explanation, and ordinary people would rage: “Doctors are great sinners and scoundrels betraying the Lord!”

The responsibility of answering such opposition forced the medical expert Simpson to compose an extensive explanatory pamphlet in 1847.

Priests condemned him by citing scripture. Today we may say scripture concerns the inner life while medicine concerns anatomy, and that invoking scripture here is inappropriate. That could not be said then. Simpson therefore searched scripture itself for a passage supporting his position and presented it in his pamphlet.

“God never said pain must not be reduced, gentlemen! He said only that childbirth causes pain, not that no means may be used to remove it. Do you know that God himself administered an anaesthetic and performed an operation? Do you realise that the first doctor to administer anaesthesia and operate was God?” Simpson asked.

The priests were alarmed. They expected Simpson to deny scripture and God's command, but he said Jesus was the first doctor to use anaesthesia and perform surgery.

“When? Upon whom? How?”

“You know only how to frighten people into believing God's commands, but do not even know the great deeds God performed. Never mind; I shall tell you. Take up the Bible. You have turned its pages many times to condemn me; look once more.

“Here is the passage in which God creates man: ‘Then the Lord caused a deep sleep to fall upon Adam; and Adam slept. He took one of his ribs and closed up the flesh.’ It is in scripture, priests! Adam underwent an operation: a bone was removed and the flesh restored. That God operated is no wonder, but attend to the words: he caused Adam to fall into a deep sleep.

“Why did he bring that deep sleep upon Adam? So that Adam would not suffer pain from the operation.

“That deep sleep is produced by anaesthesia. That is what we do. God therefore showed us the way; he was the first doctor to administer an anaesthetic and perform an operation.”

This was Simpson's argument in his pamphlet.

All this and more was required to meet religious opposition. People feared religious authorities and dreaded that defying them would bring mortal sin. Researchers endured no small hardship in removing that fear from ordinary people.

“Come sleep in the progeny-bedchamber hall! Conception is certain, and a beautiful child will be born.”

An adventurous fraud published this advertisement.

He claimed that the bed was endowed with heavenly power and that couples sleeping upon it would certainly conceive.

Was this method not more enticing than medicine, incantation, ritual immersion, and knotted cords? Demand became intense.

Couples came and paid the fee.

The place was called the Temple of Health, and the miraculous bed stood there, in 1779.

Though the man was a fraud, he seems to have possessed refined taste. He decorated the bedroom lavishly. The bed, with four legs gleaming like crystal, was made alluring enough to awaken erotic desire. Garlands made of gold-plated metal hung along its sides. Sweet music played when one lay upon it, activated by a mechanism fitted to the bed. Another device cast beautiful light upon it from the opposite side.

For a fee exceeding fifty sovereigns, a couple could spend one amorous night in the progeny-bedchamber hall.

He employed women to welcome the couples with sweet words. When they emerged after their intimacy, he gave them a feast. He created the setting for a pleasurable experience. Today the act would be punishable by law; then it was proclaimed a “miracle.”

The guardians of religion who reviled researchers' ideas as atheism made no effort to condemn the fraud who built the progeny bed. The arrangement for nights of pleasure continued without obstruction for as long as people's credulity survived.

So long as a scheme did not expose their doctrine as false in broad daylight and did not raise opposition or challenge to their domination, religious advocates destroyed no fraud's enterprise. They reserved their anger and cruelty for researchers alone.

Religious leaders who permitted the adventure called the progeny bed opposed medical methods and supported magic and incantation. Perhaps they thought that atmosphere suited them.

Today, however, their grip is weakening. People have gained much clarity, abandoned their infatuation with magic and incantation, and placed faith in medical methods.

The history of how those methods grew offers both interest and benefit. This article has been written in the hope of awakening an appetite to read it.

(*Dravida Nadu*, Pongal annual, 14 January 1956.)

---NOTES---
The translation preserves all twenty-five scan sections, the three opening time-and-place contrasts, the colloquial testimonial passages, the history of Greek and Roman medicine, the extended 1847 anaesthesia debate, the 1779 London advertisement, and the complete closing publication note.

சந்தான சப்ரமஞ்சம் combines சந்தானம் (progeny or offspring) with சப்ரமஞ்சம், a period form referring to an ornate bed or bedstead. “The Progeny Bedchamber” is used to retain both the fertility claim and the commercial room built around the bed.

சாண்டோ refers to Eugen Sandow, whose name became a period comparison for a muscular or robust physique. The article's mythological name இஸ்குலாபஸ் is rendered by the standard English form Asclepius; ஜாவஸ் is rendered Zeus. These identifications clarify transliterated names without altering the Tamil source.

The source attributes the 1847 pamphlet and biblical anaesthesia argument to “Simpson.” This is rendered as printed and is not expanded inside the translation. Historical and theological claims remain Anna's argument and have not been silently corrected or modernised.

Ten scan-backed OCR corrections were applied before translation, including the title, both 1779 references, Simpson's 1847 date, the 1956 publication date, the missing Greece-to-Rome transition, the complete bold advertisement, and three malformed Tamil words. Decorative page artefacts and non-material period spelling remain verbatim in the Tamil block.
